/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/a-plus-core2.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("a-plus-core2", [["ap1",0,1,"A small business wants its new laptops joined to an Active Directory domain and encrypted with BitLocker. Which is the lowest Windows 11 edition that supports both?",["Home","Pro","Enterprise","Education"],1,"Windows 11 Pro supports domain join and BitLocker. Home supports neither; Enterprise and Education also do, but they are higher-tier volume editions.","Objective 1.3"],["ap2",0,1,"A technician must copy a 6 GB video to a USB flash drive that has to work on both Windows and macOS. Which file system should the drive use?",["FAT32","NTFS","ext4","exFAT"],3,"exFAT is read-write on both OSs and has no 4 GB file limit. FAT32 cannot hold files over 4 GB, and macOS mounts NTFS read-only by default.","Objective 1.1"],["ap3",0,1,"A new 4 TB drive must be set up as one volume on a UEFI-based PC. Which partition style should be used?",["GPT","MBR","Extended","Dynamic spanned"],0,"GPT supports volumes far larger than 2 TB and is required for UEFI boot. MBR tops out at 2 TB.","Objective 1.2"],["ap4",0,1,"Which command repairs corrupted protected Windows system files by replacing them with cached copies?",["chkdsk /r","gpupdate /force","sfc /scannow","diskpart"],2,"System File Checker (sfc /scannow) verifies and replaces protected system files. chkdsk repairs file system errors and bad sectors, not system files.","Objective 1.5"],["ap5",0,1,"sfc /scannow reports it found corrupt files but could not repair some of them. What should the technician run next?",["DISM /Online /Cleanup-Image /RestoreHealth","gpresult /r","format C: /q","netstat -an"],0,"DISM repairs the component store that sfc uses as its source; after it finishes, run sfc again.","Objective 1.5"],["ap6",0,1,"An admin changed a Group Policy setting and wants a user's PC to apply it immediately rather than waiting. Which command should the user run?",["gpresult /r","gpupdate /force","gpresult /h report.html","whoami /groups"],1,"gpupdate /force reapplies all computer and user policies now. gpresult /r only reports which policies are applied.","Objective 1.5"],["ap7",0,1,"A technician needs to copy a large folder tree to a file server, keep permissions and resume if the network drops. Which command fits best?",["copy","xcopy","diskpart","robocopy"],3,"Robocopy handles large trees, can copy NTFS permissions (/COPYALL) and retries or restarts in restartable mode. copy and xcopy lack these features.","Objective 1.5"],["ap8",0,1,"A user wants a mapped drive letter P: that points to \\\\fs01\\projects. Which command creates it?",["net user P: \\\\fs01\\projects","diskpart assign letter=P","nslookup P: fs01","net use P: \\\\fs01\\projects"],3,"net use maps a drive letter to a network share. net user manages user accounts, and diskpart assigns letters to local volumes.","Objective 1.5"],["ap9",0,1,"A technician wants to see which programs and services run at every startup and schedule a job to run a script nightly. Which two tools should they use?",["Task Manager's Startup apps tab and Task Scheduler","Disk Management and Device Manager","Certificate Manager and Event Viewer","Registry Editor and Disk Cleanup"],0,"Task Manager shows startup impact and lets you disable startup apps; Task Scheduler runs programs on a schedule.","Objective 1.4"],["ap10",0,1,"A printer driver keeps causing errors. Which tool lets the technician roll back the driver to the previous version?",["Performance Monitor","Local Users and Groups","Device Manager","System Information"],2,"Device Manager's driver tab offers Roll Back Driver. System Information only reports hardware and driver details.","Objective 1.4"],["ap11",0,1,"A technician needs a full report of the PC's hardware, BIOS version and loaded drivers without changing anything. Which tool is best?",["msconfig","diskmgmt.msc","regedit","msinfo32"],3,"System Information (msinfo32) is a read-only report of hardware resources, components and software environment.","Objective 1.4"],["ap12",0,1,"A laptop is set to a Public network profile at a coffee shop. What does this change compared with a Private profile?",["The PC receives a static IP address","DHCP is disabled for the adapter","The VPN client connects automatically on every sign-in","Network discovery and file sharing are turned off"],3,"The Public profile hides the PC from others by disabling discovery and sharing. It does not change how the address is assigned.","Objective 1.7"],["ap13",0,1,"A user on a limited cellular hotspot complains that Windows Update used most of their data allowance. Which setting should be enabled?",["Metered connection","Hibernate after 15 minutes idle","Fast startup","Hibernate"],0,"Marking a connection as metered makes Windows defer most update downloads and background data use.","Objective 1.7"],["ap14",0,1,"A Windows PC has an IP of 192.168.10.25 but cannot reach any website by name, although pinging 8.8.8.8 works. Which setting is most likely wrong?",["Subnet mask","Default gateway","DNS server","Proxy exception list"],2,"Reaching an internet IP proves routing and the gateway work; failing only by name points to DNS.","Objective 1.7"],["ap15",0,1,"A Mac user needs automatic hourly backups of their files to an external drive. Which built-in feature should the technician set up?",["FileVault","Time Machine","Keychain Access","Spotlight"],1,"Time Machine backs up to an external or network drive automatically. FileVault is full-disk encryption.","Objective 1.8"],["ap16",0,1,"An app on a Mac has frozen and will not respond to its own Quit command. What is the quickest built-in way to close it?",["Open Disk Utility and run First Aid","Open Mission Control and swipe up","Delete the app from the Applications folder","Press Command+Option+Esc and use Force Quit"],3,"Force Quit Applications ends a hung app. Mission Control only arranges windows, and deleting the app does not stop a running process.","Objective 1.8"],["ap17",0,1,"On a Linux server, a script file must be executable by its owner only. Which command sets this?",["sudo 700 script.sh","chown 700 script.sh","chmod 777 script.sh","chmod 700 script.sh"],3,"chmod 700 gives the owner read, write and execute and removes all rights from group and others. chown changes ownership, not permissions.","Objective 1.9"],["ap18",0,1,"A technician must install a package on an Ubuntu workstation. Which command should they use?",["sudo dnf install htop","sudo apt install htop","chmod +x htop","ps htop"],1,"Ubuntu and Debian use the apt package manager, and installs need root, so sudo apt install. dnf is used on Red Hat-based distributions.","Objective 1.9"],["ap19",0,1,"Which Linux file stores the hashed user passwords?",["/etc/passwd","/etc/hosts","/etc/shadow","/etc/fstab"],2,"/etc/shadow holds password hashes and is readable only by root. /etc/passwd lists accounts but no longer stores the hashes.","Objective 1.9"],["ap20",0,1,"A user wants to install a 3D design program on a laptop running 32-bit Windows 10 with 4 GB RAM. The vendor requires a 64-bit OS and 16 GB RAM. What is the best advice?",["Install it anyway, then enlarge the page file to make up for the missing RAM","Run it in compatibility mode","Install it from an ISO instead of a download","The laptop does not meet requirements; upgrade the hardware and OS or use another PC"],3,"A 64-bit application cannot run on a 32-bit OS, and RAM below the minimum cannot be fixed by settings. The system must meet requirements.","Objective 1.10"],["ap21",0,1,"IT needs to install the same standard Windows build on 60 new desktops that support network boot. Which method is most efficient?",["Image deployment over PXE","A clean install from a DVD on each PC","An in-place upgrade on each PC","A repair installation on each PC"],0,"PXE boot with image deployment sends one prepared image to many PCs over the network, instead of installing each one by hand.","Objective 1.2"],["ap22",0,1,"A Windows 10 PC is being upgraded to Windows 11, but the upgrade check fails. Which missing hardware feature is the most likely cause on an older PC?",["An NVMe drive","TPM 2.0","A dedicated GPU","A Wi-Fi 6 adapter"],1,"Windows 11 requires TPM 2.0 and UEFI with Secure Boot capability. NVMe, a dedicated GPU and Wi-Fi 6 are not requirements.","Objective 1.2"],["ap23",0,1,"A new employee needs email, shared documents and video meetings from any device, with files synced to their laptop. What should the technician set up first?",["A local mail client with POP3 only","A mapped drive to the on-premises file server plus a local copy of Office","A user account and license in the company's cloud productivity suite","A VPN to a home router"],2,"Cloud productivity tools start with an account and license assignment, then sync and app setup on each device.","Objective 1.11"],["ap24",0,1,"Which Windows power setting saves the contents of RAM to disk and powers off completely, using no battery while off?",["Sleep","Screen saver","Fast startup","Hibernate"],3,"Hibernate writes memory to hiberfil.sys and turns the PC off. Sleep keeps RAM powered, so it still uses some battery.","Objective 1.6"],["ap25",0,1,"A technician must extend a data volume into unallocated space next to it on the same disk using a graphical tool. Which tool should they open?",["Disk Cleanup","Resource Monitor","Defragment and Optimize Drives","Disk Management"],3,"Disk Management (diskmgmt.msc) creates, formats, extends and shrinks volumes. Disk Cleanup only deletes temporary files.","Objective 1.4"],["ap26",0,2,"A user's PC shows ransom notes and many files have new extensions. According to CompTIA's malware removal procedure, what should the technician do right after verifying the symptoms?",["Educate the user","Disable System Restore","Quarantine the infected system","Run a full scan with updated definitions"],2,"The order is investigate/verify, quarantine, disable System Restore, remediate, schedule scans and updates, re-enable System Restore, educate. Isolating the PC stops the spread.","Objective 2.6"],["ap27",0,2,"During malware removal, why does the technician disable System Restore before cleaning a Windows PC?",["It prevents malware in restore points from being brought back later","It speeds up the anti-malware scan","Windows won't allow an anti-malware scan while System Restore is running","It removes the malware from memory"],0,"Restore points can hold infected files; deleting them by disabling System Restore stops reinfection from a restore.","Objective 2.6"],["ap28",0,2,"The malware has been removed and scans are scheduled. What is the final step of the procedure?",["Re-image the computer","Quarantine the PC again","Disable the user's account","Educate the end user"],3,"After re-enabling System Restore and creating a clean restore point, the last step is teaching the user how to avoid reinfection.","Objective 2.6"],["ap29",0,2,"A shared folder grants Everyone Full Control at the share level, while NTFS gives the Sales group Read. What access does a Sales user have over the network?",["Full Control","No access","Modify","Read"],3,"When share and NTFS permissions combine, the more restrictive applies, so the user gets Read.","Objective 2.2"],["ap30",0,2,"A file with NTFS permissions is moved to a different folder on the same NTFS volume. What happens to its permissions?",["They are replaced by the destination's permissions","They are reset to Everyone Full Control","The file keeps its original permissions","The file becomes read-only"],2,"A move within the same volume keeps the file's permissions. A copy, or a move to another volume, takes the destination's inherited permissions.","Objective 2.2"],["ap31",0,2,"A company wants USB flash drives encrypted with a password so data is protected if a drive is lost. Which Windows feature fits?",["EFS","Windows Hello","User Account Control","BitLocker To Go"],3,"BitLocker To Go encrypts removable drives. EFS encrypts individual files for a user on NTFS volumes, and UAC controls elevation prompts.","Objective 2.2"],["ap32",0,2,"Users often run as local administrators, and malware silently installs software. Which control best applies least privilege?",["Give users standard accounts and keep UAC enabled","Turn off Windows Defender Firewall","Enable the Guest account","Add users to the local Power Users group instead of Administrators"],0,"Standard accounts with UAC prompts for elevation stop software from installing silently with admin rights.","Objective 2.2"],["ap33",0,2,"An office replacing its Wi-Fi wants each employee to sign in with their own directory credentials instead of a shared passphrase. What should it use?",["WPA2-Personal with TKIP","WPA3-Enterprise with RADIUS","WPA3-Personal with SAE","An open network with MAC filtering"],1,"Enterprise mode uses 802.1X with a RADIUS server so each user authenticates individually. Personal modes use one shared passphrase.","Objective 2.3"],["ap34",0,2,"Which authentication protocol uses tickets issued by a key distribution center and is used by Active Directory domain logons?",["RADIUS","TACACS+","Kerberos","Diameter"],2,"Kerberos issues time-limited tickets from the domain controller's KDC. RADIUS and TACACS+ are AAA protocols for network access and device administration.","Objective 2.3"],["ap35",0,2,"Network admins need an AAA protocol for logging in to switches and routers that encrypts the whole payload and separates authorization from authentication. Which protocol fits?",["LDAP","RADIUS","Kerberos","TACACS+"],3,"TACACS+ encrypts the full packet and handles authentication, authorization and accounting separately, which suits device administration. RADIUS encrypts only the password.","Objective 2.3"],["ap36",0,2,"A user's PC shows no symptoms, but a tool detects that a hidden kernel-level program is concealing processes from Task Manager. What type of malware is this?",["Adware","Keylogger","Rootkit","Cryptominer"],2,"Rootkits hide at a low level, often the kernel or boot process, and conceal their files and processes from normal tools.","Objective 2.4"],["ap37",0,2,"A PC's CPU runs near 100% even when idle, the fan is loud and there is no obvious app open. Which malware is most likely?",["Spyware","Ransomware","Boot sector virus","Cryptominer"],3,"Cryptominers use CPU or GPU cycles to mine cryptocurrency, causing constant high load. Ransomware would encrypt files and demand payment.","Objective 2.4"],["ap38",0,2,"An employee gets a text message saying their payroll account is locked, with a link to sign in. What attack is this?",["Vishing","Whaling","Smishing","Tailgating"],2,"Smishing is phishing by SMS. Vishing uses voice calls, and whaling targets senior executives.","Objective 2.5"],["ap39",0,2,"A poster in the break room has a QR code offering a free lunch coupon, which leads to a fake company sign-in page. What is this?",["Evil twin","QR code phishing","Dumpster diving","Shoulder surfing"],1,"QR code phishing hides a malicious URL in a QR code so users can't easily inspect the link before scanning.","Objective 2.5"],["ap40",0,2,"An attacker sets up a hotspot in the lobby with the same name as the company guest Wi-Fi to capture traffic. What is this?",["Zero-day exploit","Brute-force attack","SQL injection","Evil twin"],3,"An evil twin is a rogue access point that copies a legitimate SSID so users connect to it.","Objective 2.5"],["ap41",0,2,"The accounts payable team receives an email that appears to come from a regular supplier's real mailbox, asking them to change the bank account for invoice payments. What attack is this?",["Distributed denial of service","Cross-site scripting","Business email compromise","Impersonation by telephone"],2,"BEC uses a compromised or spoofed business mailbox to redirect payments. Verify any bank change by calling a known number.","Objective 2.5"],["ap42",0,2,"Old hard disk drives with customer data must be destroyed so that a reuse vendor can't recover anything, and the company needs proof. What is the best approach?",["Run a full format on every drive and then donate them all to a local school","Delete the partitions with diskpart","Move the files to the Recycle Bin and empty it","Have a certified vendor shred them and issue a certificate of destruction"],3,"Physical shredding makes the data unrecoverable, and a certificate of destruction documents it. Formatting and deleting leave recoverable data.","Objective 2.8"],["ap43",0,2,"A technician plans to degauss a batch of retired SSDs. Why is this a poor choice?",["SSDs store data in flash memory, not magnetically","Degaussing voids the warranty","Degaussing only works on encrypted drives","SSDs must be formatted and partitioned before they can be degaussed"],0,"Degaussing destroys magnetic media such as HDDs and tapes. SSDs need secure erase, crypto-erase or physical destruction.","Objective 2.8"],["ap44",0,2,"Which change to a new SOHO router is most important to do first?",["Enable UPnP for gaming consoles","Hide the SSID","Change the default administrator password","Turn on WPS so new devices can pair with one button press"],2,"Default admin credentials are publicly known. UPnP and WPS weaken security, and hiding the SSID adds little protection.","Objective 2.9"],["ap45",0,2,"A home user wants a security camera app reachable from the internet on TCP 8443 through the router. What must be configured?",["DHCP reservation only","Content filtering for the camera's web traffic","Port forwarding to the camera's IP","A guest network"],2,"Port forwarding sends inbound traffic on a port to an internal host; pair it with a DHCP reservation so the address doesn't change.","Objective 2.9"],["ap46",0,2,"A user downloaded an installer from a mirror site. How can they confirm the file was not altered?",["Check that the file name and icon match the product name","Compare its hash with the one published by the vendor","Check that the download used HTTP","Right-click it and choose Run as administrator"],1,"A matching SHA-256 hash proves the file is identical to the one the vendor published.","Objective 2.10"],["ap47",0,2,"A company wants lost corporate phones to be locatable and erasable, and wants to enforce screen locks and encryption on every device. What should it deploy?",["A VPN client on every phone","A password manager","Mobile device management","Bluetooth pairing"],2,"MDM enforces policies such as passcodes and encryption and supports locate and remote wipe.","Objective 2.7"],["ap48",0,2,"Which workstation hardening step stops malware on an inserted USB drive from launching automatically?",["Enable remote desktop","Enable the Guest account","Turn off screen saver locks","Disable AutoRun/AutoPlay"],3,"Disabling AutoRun and AutoPlay stops media from executing content when it is inserted.","Objective 2.7"],["ap49",0,2,"Employees must badge in through a door, wait in a small room until that door closes, and then badge again through a second door. What is this control?",["Bollard","Access control vestibule","Badge reader with motion sensor","Video surveillance"],1,"An access control vestibule (mantrap) lets only one door open at a time, which stops tailgating.","Objective 2.1"],["ap50",0,2,"A company wants to block employees from emailing files that contain credit card numbers outside the organization. Which control fits?",["Data loss prevention","Single sign-on","Directory services with group policy","Screen lock policy"],0,"DLP inspects outgoing data for sensitive patterns such as card numbers and blocks or flags it.","Objective 2.1"],["ap51",0,3,"After a graphics driver update, a Windows PC shows a blue screen during every normal boot. What should the technician do first?",["Replace the video card","Boot into Safe Mode and roll back the driver","Reinstall Windows from scratch","Run chkdsk /r from the desktop after the next reboot"],1,"Safe Mode loads basic drivers, so the faulty driver can be rolled back. Reinstalling or replacing hardware is premature.","Objective 3.1"],["ap52",0,3,"A PC started crashing after this week's Windows cumulative update. The user needs it working today. What is the best fix?",["Uninstall the latest quality update from Windows Update or WinRE","Disable Windows Update permanently","Reimage the PC with no updates","Replace the RAM"],0,"Rolling back the problem update fixes the regression quickly. Disabling updates permanently leaves the PC exposed.","Objective 3.1"],["ap53",0,3,"A service required by an accounting app fails to start after reboots, and Event Viewer shows a dependency failed. What should the technician check?",["The screen resolution","The print spooler queue and the default printer setting","The services that it depends on in services.msc","The user's wallpaper settings"],2,"A service won't start if a service it depends on is stopped or disabled; the Dependencies tab lists them.","Objective 3.1"],["ap54",0,3,"A user's PC is very slow. Task Manager shows memory at 98% and heavy disk activity while several large apps are open. What is the best long-term fix?",["Run Disk Cleanup","Add more RAM","Defragment the SSD","Disable the page file"],1,"Constant high memory use forces paging to disk, which causes the slowness. More RAM fixes the root cause; disabling the page file would cause crashes.","Objective 3.1"],["ap55",0,3,"A PC displays \"Operating system not found\" after a technician added a second drive. Nothing else changed. What is the most likely cause?",["The boot order now lists the new drive first","The monitor cable is loose","The Windows license expired when the hardware changed","The DNS server is down"],0,"Adding a drive can change the firmware boot order; set the OS drive first in UEFI/BIOS.","Objective 3.1"],["ap56",0,3,"A user's clock keeps drifting several minutes, and they can't log on to the domain until it is corrected. What should the technician check?",["The display refresh rate","Whether the Windows Time service syncs to a valid NTP source","The size of the page file","The browser's pop-up blocker"],1,"Kerberos rejects logons when clocks differ too much. Windows Time must sync (w32tm /resync), and a failing CMOS battery can also cause drift.","Objective 3.1"],["ap57",0,3,"Only one user on a shared PC gets a temporary profile at every sign-in and loses their desktop settings. Other users are fine. What is the best fix?",["Reinstall Windows","Replace the hard drive","Rebuild the user's profile","Reset the BIOS to defaults"],2,"A corrupt profile causes temporary profiles for that user only. Back up the data, then rebuild the profile.","Objective 3.1"],["ap58",0,3,"Windows warns that a USB controller has run out of resources when a user connects a dock, webcam and two drives to one hub. What should they try?",["Update the BIOS password","Spread devices across USB ports on different controllers","Disable Windows Firewall","Change the power plan to Balanced"],1,"Each USB controller has limited endpoints; moving devices to ports on other controllers (or removing the hub) clears the warning.","Objective 3.1"],["ap59",0,3,"A phone app closes immediately every time it is opened. Restarting the phone did not help. What should the technician try next?",["Clear the app's cache and data or reinstall it","Factory reset the phone","Replace the battery","Enable airplane mode"],0,"Clearing the app's cache or reinstalling it fixes corrupt app data without the data loss of a factory reset.","Objective 3.2"],["ap60",0,3,"A user's phone battery now lasts half a day. Battery settings show one social app used most of the power in the background. What is the best fix?",["Replace the phone","Restrict the app's background activity or update it","Turn off Wi-Fi permanently","Lower the screen brightness to zero and turn on dark mode"],1,"Battery usage shows the culprit; limiting background refresh or updating the misbehaving app addresses it directly.","Objective 3.2"],["ap61",0,3,"A tablet's screen won't rotate when turned sideways in any app. What should be checked first?",["Whether rotation lock is enabled","The Bluetooth pairing list","The storage space available","The Wi-Fi band"],0,"Rotation lock in the quick settings keeps the screen in one orientation; it is the most common cause.","Objective 3.2"],["ap62",0,3,"A user installed an app from a website link instead of the official store. Now the phone shows fake virus warnings and uses far more data. What is the most likely cause?",["A failing battery","A sideloaded malicious app","A weak Wi-Fi signal forcing cellular data","An expired SIM card"],1,"Apps sideloaded from outside the official store skip vetting and often carry malware that causes fake alerts and extra traffic.","Objective 3.3"],["ap63",0,3,"An employee's phone was rooted to install custom themes. Why is this a security concern for the company?",["Rooting speeds up the processor","Rooting blocks all app updates","Rooting bypasses OS security controls and can break MDM enforcement","Rooting disables Bluetooth"],2,"Rooting or jailbreaking removes built-in sandboxing and restrictions, letting apps gain full control and letting users avoid MDM policy.","Objective 3.3"],["ap64",0,3,"A user's browser shows a certificate warning on their bank's site, but only on the office network. Other sites are fine. What should the technician suspect?",["The browser cache is full","The site is being intercepted or the proxy is misconfigured","The browser's saved passwords for the bank have expired and need resetting","The PC needs more RAM"],1,"A certificate mismatch on one network can mean an on-path attack or a misconfigured inspecting proxy. Users should not click through it.","Objective 3.4"],["ap65",0,3,"Every search in a user's browser goes to an unfamiliar search site, and a new toolbar appeared. What should the technician do first?",["Reinstall the operating system","Replace the network card","Change the user's email password","Remove unknown extensions and reset the browser's search and home settings"],3,"Redirection and toolbars usually come from a malicious extension or hijacked settings. Remove them and then scan for malware.","Objective 3.4"],["ap66",0,3,"A pop-up claims the PC is infected and tells the user to call a support number. The installed antivirus shows no alerts. What is this most likely?",["A real Windows Defender alert","A genuine warning from the PC's hardware diagnostics","A fake security alert (scareware)","A Windows Update notice"],2,"Fake antivirus alerts push users to call scammers or install malware. Close the browser and run a scan with the real antivirus.","Objective 3.4"],["ap67",0,3,"After a malware cleanup, a PC still can't reach any websites, though it has a valid IP. The technician finds a proxy set to 127.0.0.1:8080. What should they do?",["Remove the proxy setting and check the hosts file","Replace the Ethernet cable","Renew the DHCP lease only","Disable the firewall"],0,"Malware often sets a rogue proxy or hosts entries to hijack traffic. Clear them after removal.","Objective 3.4"],["ap68",0,3,"Windows updates keep failing on a PC that recently had malware, and some system files appear renamed. What is the most likely explanation?",["The update server is offline for everyone","Malware altered system files and blocked updates","The PC is on a metered connection","The time zone is set incorrectly"],1,"Malware often tampers with system files and update services to avoid being removed. Run sfc and DISM, or reimage if it can't be trusted.","Objective 3.4"],["ap69",0,3,"An app crashes several times a day. Where can the technician see a timeline of app failures and Windows changes to find what changed?",["Reliability Monitor","Disk Management","Device Manager","Local Security Policy"],0,"Reliability Monitor charts crashes, failures and installs by day, making it easy to connect a crash to a recent change.","Objective 3.1"],["ap70",0,3,"A laptop shuts off without warning, mostly while gaming, and Event Viewer shows Kernel-Power errors. What should be checked first?",["The user's password age","Overheating: vents, fans and thermal readings","The screen brightness and display timeout settings","The Windows edition"],1,"Unexpected shutdowns under load often come from overheating protection. Clean vents and check fans and temperatures.","Objective 3.1"],["ap71",0,3,"A user can't connect wireless earbuds to their phone, though they worked yesterday. Bluetooth is on. What is a good first step?",["Factory reset the phone","Remove (forget) the earbuds and pair them again","Disable Wi-Fi calling","Install an app from a third-party store"],1,"Forgetting the device and re-pairing clears a bad pairing record; it is quick and non-destructive.","Objective 3.2"],["ap72",0,4,"A technician is closing a ticket. What should the resolution notes include?",["Only the word \"fixed\"","The user's password","A clear description of the problem, steps taken and final fix","A personal opinion about how the user probably caused the problem"],2,"Good notes let others understand what happened and reuse the fix. Never record passwords in tickets.","Objective 4.1"],["ap73",0,4,"IT needs a central record of every laptop, who has it, its warranty date and its configuration. What should it use?",["An asset management database (CMDB) with asset tags","A shared desktop wallpaper","The DHCP lease table","A printed phone list"],0,"Asset management with tags and a CMDB tracks devices, assigned users, warranty and configuration.","Objective 4.1"],["ap74",0,4,"A new hire's first day requires an account, a laptop, email and building access. Which document keeps this consistent every time?",["An incident report","An acceptable use policy","A service-level agreement","An onboarding checklist"],3,"An onboarding (new user setup) checklist lists every step so nothing is missed. The AUP defines allowed use of systems.","Objective 4.1"],["ap75",0,4,"Before a planned firewall change, the change advisory board asks what will happen if the change breaks production. What part of the request answers this?",["The rollback plan","The asset tag","The end-user acceptance","The purchase order"],0,"A rollback plan explains how to return to the previous working state if the change fails.","Objective 4.2"],["ap76",0,4,"A backup job runs a full backup on Sunday and differential backups the other days. The server fails Thursday morning. What must be restored?",["Only Wednesday's differential","Sunday's full and every differential since","Sunday's full and Wednesday's differential","Only Sunday's full"],2,"A differential holds all changes since the last full, so you need the full plus the latest differential.","Objective 4.3"],["ap77",0,4,"A company wants to follow the 3-2-1 backup rule. Which setup meets it?",["Three copies of the data on two types of media, with one copy off-site","Three backups a day kept for two weeks, one per server","Three servers in two racks with one UPS","One full, two incremental and three differential backups"],0,"3-2-1 means three copies, two different media, one off-site.","Objective 4.3"],["ap78",0,4,"Backups have run nightly for a year, but nobody knows if they work. What should the team do?",["Switch to incremental backups","Buy more backup storage","Perform regular test restores","Delete older backups"],2,"Only a restore test proves backups are usable and meet recovery time goals.","Objective 4.3"],["ap79",0,4,"Before replacing RAM in a desktop, what should a technician do to prevent electrostatic damage?",["Wear rubber gloves only","Leave the PC plugged in and powered on","Use a vacuum to clean the slots","Wear an ESD wrist strap connected to ground and use an ESD mat"],3,"An ESD strap and mat equalize charge so static doesn't discharge into components. Unplug the PC before working inside.","Objective 4.4"],["ap80",0,4,"A technician needs to know how to safely handle and dispose of a toner cartridge that leaked. Where should they look?",["The product's safety data sheet (SDS)","The printer's IP configuration","The service-level agreement with the printer vendor","The EULA"],0,"The SDS lists hazards, handling, spill clean-up and disposal instructions for a product.","Objective 4.5"],["ap81",0,4,"An office often has brief power flickers that reboot PCs and have corrupted a server's database. What should be installed?",["A surge suppressor only","A KVM switch","An uninterruptible power supply","A second monitor"],2,"A UPS provides battery power through short outages and allows a clean shutdown. A surge suppressor does not supply power.","Objective 4.5"],["ap82",0,4,"While repairing a laptop, a technician sees files that appear to be illegal content. What should they do first?",["Delete the files and finish the repair","Stop, leave the evidence untouched and follow the incident process by notifying the proper authority","Copy the files to a USB drive for later","Tell coworkers about it"],1,"Preserve evidence, report through policy (management or law enforcement) and keep chain of custody. Deleting or copying damages the evidence.","Objective 4.6"],["ap83",0,4,"A company has a license for 25 installs of an app and has installed it on 40 PCs. What does this violate?",["The software's license agreement (EULA)","The acceptable use policy of the ISP","GDPR","The service-level agreement with the help desk"],0,"The EULA sets how many installs or users are allowed; going beyond it is a licensing violation.","Objective 4.6"],["ap84",0,4,"A technician is on site to fix a slow PC and the customer is visibly angry about repeated problems. What is the best approach?",["Explain it's not your fault and move on","Argue about who caused the issue","Post about it on social media","Listen without interrupting, acknowledge the frustration and explain the next steps"],3,"Active listening, staying calm and setting clear expectations help settle a difficult customer.","Objective 4.7"],["ap85",0,4,"A technician must remap the same network drives on 300 Windows PCs every time users sign in. Which script type fits best?",["A PowerShell (.ps1) script","A Bash (.sh) script","A macOS .dmg file","A Python notebook on the technician's phone"],0,"PowerShell is native to Windows and suits logon scripts that map drives. Bash scripts are for Linux and macOS shells.","Objective 4.8"],["ap86",0,4,"A script downloaded from a forum claims to speed up Windows. What is the main risk of running it on production PCs without review?",["It may be too short","It could introduce malware or change system settings unintentionally","It will switch every PC to the wrong keyboard layout and display language","It cannot run without Python"],1,"Untested scripts can carry malware, alter settings or crash systems. Review and test in a sandbox first.","Objective 4.8"],["ap87",0,4,"A help desk needs to monitor, patch and remotely support hundreds of client PCs from one console. Which tool type fits?",["Remote monitoring and management (RMM)","VNC on each PC with a shared password","Telnet","Open RDP ports on each PC to the internet"],0,"RMM platforms centralize monitoring, patching, scripting and remote control. Exposing RDP or Telnet to the internet is unsafe.","Objective 4.9"],["ap88",0,4,"An admin needs secure command-line access to a Linux server across the network. Which protocol should they use?",["RDP","SSH","Telnet","SPICE"],1,"SSH provides an encrypted remote shell. Telnet sends everything, including passwords, in clear text.","Objective 4.9"],["ap89",0,4,"An employee pastes a customer contract into a free public AI chatbot to summarize it. What is the main concern?",["The summary will be too short","Chatbots cannot read contracts","Confidential data may be stored or used by the public model's provider","The contract will be deleted from the server"],2,"Public AI tools may retain and use submitted data, which risks privacy and confidentiality. Company policy should restrict this.","Objective 4.10"],["ap90",0,4,"An AI assistant gives a technician a command with a switch that does not exist. What AI limitation is this?",["Bias","Plagiarism","Data retention","Hallucination"],3,"A hallucination is confident but false output. AI answers must be checked for accuracy before use.","Objective 4.10"]]);
